 Dividing the cited remainder bounds by $\gamma^2$ gives $O(H+\gamma^{-1/2})$, or $O(H^2+H\gamma^{-1/2})$, neither of which guarantees identification of the second-order coefficient. Under the additional regularity and noise assumptions of Section~\ref{sec:setting}, subtracting both explicit bias terms from our expansion and dividing by $\gamma^2$ instead leaves $O_{H,d}(1/N+\gamma)$, uniformly in $N$ for fixed $d,H$. This residual vanishes along every joint limit $\gamma\to0$, $N\to\infty$. The resulting formulas specify the direction, magnitude, and local-step dependence of the client-independent second-order contribution, and separate the part shared with ordinary local updates from the additional persistent-control contribution. This sharper characterization concerns the shared submodel; the prior results retain their broader coverage of heterogeneous objectives and state-dependent noise.
 
+The leading stationary covariance of the persistent controls is also
+already explicit in \citet[Lemma~5.1]{mangold2025scaffold}; on the shared
+submodel it equals $(1-1/N)C/H+O_H(\gamma)$. Its contribution to
+within-round second moments provides a direct precursor to the additional
+coefficient isolated here. The claimed refinement is the explicit
+matched stationary increment together with the $N$-uniform remainder,
+which requires resolving terms absorbed into the earlier error bounds.
+
 The controlled experiments in Section~\ref{sec:num-controlled} assess these newly resolved quantitative predictions: the algorithm-specific coefficients, their matched difference, and its local-step dependence. The earlier upper bounds remain compatible with the observations but do not specify these coefficient targets. Thus, the experiments examine the additional information supplied by the analysis, not a reduction in bias obtained by modifying either algorithm.
 
